model_agreement.summary:
The short range is tight. Every model is dry and mild today and builds a 1033-1039 hPa high over Scotland on 2-5 November: ECMWF, ICON, GEM and AIFS centre it over north-east Scotland on 5 November, while JMA and NAVGEM have it over the North Sea. The only disagreement is drizzle from low cloud on 3, 5 and 6 November. GEM ENS, UKMO ENS, AIGEFS and ICON-EPS have it; the ECMWF family and GEFS are mostly dry. I side with the dry group but flag drizzle on 3 and 6 November.

For temperatures I start from the bias-corrected blend, where ICON has the top weight to day 4 (temperature error 0.6-0.9 °C). Against the airport thermometer, nearly every model and my own past forecasts run highs too low (mine by 0.7-1.3 °C) and lows too high on clear nights. So I nudge highs to the top of the blend and keep clear-night lows a little lower.

From 8 November the spread widens:
- ECMWF HRES and JMA bring a deep Atlantic low south-west of Ireland;
- GEM and AIFS are gentler south-westerlies;
- the ensemble means sit between, with a trough just west of the UK.

stratosphere.winter_impact:
A major reversal in the first week of November is almost unprecedented. It looks short at 10 hPa, roughly 5-14 November, but it is deep on the 100 hPa polar cap. The troposphere is already going that way: GEFS forecasts the AO at -1.4 and the NAO at about -1 by 10-13 November. Much of that comes from the same Ural-Scandinavian block that drove the wave pulse, not from a downward response. About two thirds of events like this couple down, typically 1-3 weeks later. That puts Edinburgh's window for extra blocking and colder interludes at about mid-November to early December, and ECMWF's blocking regime rising to about 40% on 3-7 December fits that.

Several things temper it:
- the 50 hPa wind stays westerly;
- the QBO is westerly (+12.9 m/s at 30 hPa);
- the wave driving collapses after 8 November;
- ECMWF rebuilds the vortex to above normal (about 42 m/s) by mid-December, which would favour mild, wet westerlies later in December;
- the cold source upwind is thin: at most 8% of ECMWF members have -8 °C at 850 hPa over Stockholm or Oslo by 14 November, with almost no snow cover;
- the North Sea is +1.1 °C above its 1991-2020 normal (58% of cells above +1 °C, OISST).

For the city, a blocked spell in the next month most likely means frost, fog and raw, grey easterlies, or a chilly north-westerly with snow showers on the Pentlands, rather than lowland snow.
